+# CSV contact import
+
+## Why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets and wants to load a whole CSV file into contactbook
+instead of adding contacts one by one. The import must tell them exactly what happened:
+which contacts were created, which were updated, and which rows had problems, by the
+row number they see in Excel. Problems in one row should not block the rest of the
+file. Files that can't be imported at all (wrong format, wrong encoding, too big) are
+rejected whole, with a message the user can act on.
+
+## Interface (fixed)
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Row number**: the spreadsheet row number Excel shows. The header is row 1 and the first
+  contact is row 2. Every line counts, blank lines included. A quoted cell that contains
+  line breaks is still one row.
+- **Blank row**: an empty line, or a row where every cell is empty or only spaces (for
+  example the `,,,` lines Excel leaves behind).
+- **Contact row**: any row after the header that is not blank.
+- **`column`** in a `RowIssue` is one of `name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`, or `None` when
+  the issue isn't about a single field.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Input and encoding
+1. Bytes are decoded as UTF-8, and a leading UTF-8 BOM (as Excel's "CSV UTF-8" writes) is ignored.
+2. Text input that still starts with the BOM character (U+FEFF) is handled the same as text without it.
+3. Bytes that aren't valid UTF-8 raise `ImportFileError`, and its message tells the user to
+   re-save the file as "CSV UTF-8".
+4. Input that is empty or only whitespace raises `ImportFileError`.
+5. Line endings `\n`, `\r\n` and `\r` are all accepted.
+6. Only comma is accepted as the delimiter. Cells follow standard CSV quoting, so quoted cells may
+   contain commas, quotes and line breaks.
+
+### Header
+7. Column names are matched ignoring case and surrounding spaces, and columns may come in any order.
+8. The name comes from a `name` column. If there's no `name` column but both `first_name` and
+   `last_name` are present, the name is first and last joined by one space. If only one of them
+   is non-empty, that one is the name. Name problems in such a file are still reported with
+   column `name`.
+9. `email` is required, and so is a name source (`name`, or both `first_name` and `last_name`).
+   If either is missing, `ImportFileError` is raised and names the missing column.
+10. `phone` and `tags` columns are optional.
+11. The same column name appearing twice in the header raises `ImportFileError`.
+12. Other columns are ignored. Each one produces one warning on row 1 whose message names it.
+13. A file that is exactly the same as `export_csv` output can be imported, and doing so
+    recreates the same contacts.
+
+### File-level limits and atomicity
+14. A file with more than 1000 contact rows raises `ImportFileError`. Blank rows don't count,
+    so exactly 1000 contact rows is accepted.
+15. A file with only a header (or a header followed only by blank rows) returns an empty report
+    and leaves the repo unchanged.
+16. Whenever `ImportFileError` is raised, the repo is left completely unchanged.
+17. Blank rows are skipped silently: no error, no warning.
+
+### Rows: new contacts
+18. A valid row whose email isn't in the repo creates a contact. Its normalised email goes in
+    `created`, and all values are normalised the same way as `Contact.create`.
+19. The tags cell holds tags separated by `;`. Empty pieces (`a;;b`, a trailing `;`) are ignored.
+
+### Rows: existing contacts (merge)
+20. A valid row whose email (case-insensitive) is already in the repo updates that contact. It
+    goes in `updated` and is never an error.
+21. The file's name replaces the stored name.
+22. The file's phone replaces the stored phone only when the cell has a value. An empty cell, or
+    no `phone` column, never clears a stored phone.
+23. The file's tags are added to the stored tags. An import never removes tags.
+24. If merging changes nothing, the email is in neither `created` nor `updated`, and the stored
+    contact is left alone.
+
+### Row errors (row rejected, nothing from it applied)
+25. A missing or invalid email is an error with column `email`.
+26. A missing or invalid name is an error with column `name`. Name is required on every row,
+    including rows for existing contacts.
+27. Any invalid tag is an error with column `tags`.
+28. A rejected row changes nothing in the repo, even when its email belongs to an existing contact.
+29. A row with several bad fields among name, email and tags gets one error for each bad field.
+30. A row with more non-empty cells than the header has columns is an error with column `None`.
+    Extra cells that are empty are ignored. A row with fewer cells than the header treats the
+    missing cells as empty.
+31. A row whose normalised email matches an earlier **accepted** row in the same file is an
+    error with column `email`, and its message names the earlier row number. The first
+    accepted row wins.
+32. Error `message`s are human-readable and give the reason. Validation messages come from
+    `validation.py`.
+
+### Row warnings (row still applied)
+33. An invalid phone doesn't reject the row. The rest of the row is applied, the phone stays as
+    it was (no phone for a new contact), and a warning with column `phone` is added.
+34. Phone warnings are only reported for rows that weren't rejected.
+
+### Report
+35. `created`, `updated`, `errors` and `warnings` are listed in row order. Within a row, issues
+    appear in header-column order. Header warnings (row 1) come first.
+36. Each email appears at most once across `created` and `updated`.
+37. Valid rows are imported even when other rows in the same file have errors.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- No CLI command or HTTP endpoint; this is the core function only.
+- No dry-run or preview mode.
+- Contacts that aren't in the file are never deleted.
+- Formats other than CSV, delimiters other than comma, and encodings other than UTF-8
+  (no Windows-1252 fallback, no auto-detection).
+- An import never removes tags and never clears a phone.
+
+## Decisions made without explicit sign-off
+
+These came up while writing the spec. Each one is a judgement call that's easy to change:
+
+- An email repeated in the file is a duplicate only of an earlier *accepted* row (31). If the first
+  occurrence was rejected, a later valid row with that email is still imported.
+- Extra cells that are empty (trailing commas) are ignored instead of being an error (30).
+- Rejected rows don't also report a phone warning (34).
+- Unknown columns produce a warning instead of being silently ignored (12).
+- With `first_name`/`last_name`, a row that has only one of them filled uses that one as the name (8).
+- If both `name` and `first_name`/`last_name` are present, `name` is used and the other two get the
+  unknown-column warning.
